    "https://jobs.du.edu/jobs/faculty-developer-office-of-teaching-and-learning-denver-co-colorado-united-states": {
      "title": "Faculty Developer, Office of Teaching and Learning",
      "description": "Faculty Developer, Office of Teaching and Learning - Denver, CO, Colorado, United States Jobs@DU Benefits Compensation Community Support and Engagement Need Help? My Profile Kiosk mode Your Work Matters Here. At DU, every role contributes to a community where students and employees can grow, connect, find purpose, and thrive. In compliance with the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA), the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, and University of Denver policy, DU provides reasonable accommodations to provide equal access to job opportunities, job performance, and workplace benefits. Visit DU Accessibility to learn more about reasonable accommodations and accessibility. To request reasonable accommodations please contact the ADA Coordinator in The Office of Civil Rights & Equal Opportunity (CREO) at ADA.Coordinator@du.edu or by calling 303-871-3941. Faculty Developer, Office of Teaching and Learning 499084 Denver, CO, Colorado Staff Full-Time Closing on: Nov 1 2026 Add to favorites View favorites Located within the Vice Provost of Faculty Success within the Office of the Provost, the current mission of the Office of Teaching and Learning is to support faculty in the advancement of transformative, inclusive, and impactful teaching and learning across all disciplines and modalities to actualize DU’s educational promise to all students The OTL staff supports faculty by advancing skills in course design, pedagogy,  inclusive teaching, Scholarship of Teaching and Learning and academic assessment. We seek a highly organized, self-motivated, tech-savvy, solution-oriented, and customer-focused individual to serve as Faculty Developer. Position Summary The Faculty Developer reports to the Assistant Vice Provost/Director of the Office of Teaching and Learning (OTL) and works collaboratively with OTL staff members and university faculty. The primary function of the Faculty Developer is to support the adoption and effective use of educational technology through faculty development offerings and consultations. Additionally, the Faculty Developer provides the first level of support for troubleshooting educational technology issues with faculty and staff. This is a full-time staff position. Essential Functions Faculty Development Programs and Initiatives Design, develop, implement, and assess research-informed faculty development programs, surrounding online learning pedagogy, educational technology, and emerging technologies. Support faculty and staff in the adoption and use of learning technologies in pedagogically effective ways. This includes but is not limited to faculty development courses, webinars, faculty learning communities, self-paced workshops, and other specialized offerings as needs arise. Work in collaboration with IT to support the administration of the Learning Management System (Canvas), including changes with the LMS, integrations, policies and guidelines, technical challenges, developing and delivering training, and best practices. Consultations Conduct confidential and problem-solving consultations with faculty members and academic departments about teaching and learning issues, particularly as it pertains to online learning, educational technology, and emergent teaching trends. Provide technical support and troubleshooting for the learning management system and other instructional technologies supported by the university. Provide support for individuals and departments as they explore solutions for evolving teaching needs through the adoption of new educational technologies. Conduct confidential and problem-solving consultations with faculty members and academic departments about teaching and learning issues, particularly as it pertains to online learning and the LMS. Provide technical support and troubleshooting for the learning management system and other instructional technologies supported by the university. Collaboration within and beyond the OTL Participate as a collaborative team member and provide thought leadership in areas of expertise through support and implementation of OTL activities and programs Collaborate with OTL staff to support initiatives related to new faculty onboarding, ongoing academic year programming, and others as needed. Represent the OTL and provide expertise to campus-wide initiatives and participate in campus-wide committees that intersect with the role. Provides backup technical support to OTL-supported educational technology systems as needed. Other duties as assigned by supervisor National Trends and Practice Maintain currency in the field of faculty development, educational and emergent technology, and online education Contribute to national dialogue and scholarship by participating in national and/or local conferences and engaging in research necessary to share DU’s achievements with the broader scholarly community. Seek out professional development, and actively participate in relevant professional organizations (as funding allows). Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities Knowledge of emerging trends in higher education related to educational technology and online learning Proficiency with Learning Management System administration Knowledge of trends and effective faculty development practices Ability to create inclusive learning environments Knowledge of teaching trends in adult learning Ability to work collaboratively with faculty, staff, and students Ability to work independently and with minimal supervision Ability to manage competing priorities and deadlines Public speaking and presentations Effective written and oral communication Required Qualifications Masters degree. Preferred Qualifications Experience supporting the administration of an academic LMS platform Experience working in a higher education setting Expertise in instructional design. Working Environment Standard office environment. Unexpected interruptions occur often and stress level is moderate to high. Noise level is quiet to moderate. Physical Activities Ability to work in front of a computer for extended periods of time. Occasionally required to move about the office/campus. Work Schedule Monday - Friday, 8:00 a.m. - 4:30 p.m.  In accordance with the University’s flexible work policy, this position is eligible to be considered for partial remote work. Further details regarding this plan will be determined with the hiring manager and are dependent on the division and team specific needs. Application Deadline For consideration, please submit your application materials by 4:00 p.m. (MST) November 1, 2026. Special Instructions Candidates must apply online through jobs.du.edu to be considered. Only applications submitted online will be accepted. Salary Grade Number: The salary grade for the position is 10. Salary Range: The salary range for this position is $66,000 - $72,000. The University of Denver has provided a compensation range that represents its good faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting. The University may ultimately pay more or less than the posted compensation range. The salary offered to the selected candidate will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information, but not based on a candidate’s sex or any other protected status. Benefits: The University of Denver offers excellent benefits , including medical, dental, retirement, paid time off, tuition benefit and ECO pass. The University of Denver is a private institution that empowers students who want to make a difference. Learn more about the University of Denver . Please include the following documents with your application: 1. Resume 2. Cover Letter The University of Denver is an equal opportunity employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex (including sex stereotypes, sex characteristics, sexual orientation, gender identity, and gender expression), marital, family, or parental status, pregnancy or related conditions, national origin, disability, or status as a protected veteran. The University of Denver does not discriminate and prohibits discrimination on the basis of race, color, national origin, ancestry, age, religion, creed, disability, sex (including sex stereotypes, sex characteristics, sexual orientation, gender identity, and gender expression), marital family, and parental status, pregnancy, genetic information, military enlistment, or veteran status, and any other class of individuals protected from discrimination under federal, state, or local law, regulation, or ordinance in any of the University’s educational programs and activities, and in the employment (including application for employment) and admissions (including application for admission) context, as required by Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972; the Americans with Disabilities Act; Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973; Title VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964; the Age Discrimination Act of 1975; the Age Discrimination in Employment Act of 1967; the Equal Pay Act; the Colorado Equal Pay for Equal Work Act; the Colorado Protecting Opportunities and Worker's Rights (\"POWR\") Act; and any other federal, state, and local laws, regulations, or ordinances that prohibit discrimination, harassment, and/or retaliation. For more information, please see the University of Denver's Non‑Discrimination‑Statement . All offers of employment are based upon satisfactory completion of a criminal history and National Sex Offender Registry background check. Back to job search Apply Now First Name (required) 3bd8947a Last Name (required) 2bd001d6 Email (required) c41bdc8b Refer someone to this job Apply Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you Recommended Jobs Research Lab Coordinator, Department of Psychology Denver, CO, Colorado Lab Administration/Management, Research The Research Lab Coordinator will play a central role in launching and managing the Self-Regulation, Adaptation, and Growing Experiences (SAGE) Lab at the University of Denver, directed by Dr. Meriah DeJoseph.  Primary responsibilities include assisting with study development and protocol implementation, managing IRB submissions, recruiting and scheduling participants, coordinating data collection, overseeing research assistants, and managing and cleaning data. Building Services Specialist Denver, CO, Colorado Facilities Management & Planning, Maintenance / Grounds / Skilled Trades Under the general direct supervision of the Conversion Manager and the general direction of the Operations Manager of Auxiliary Facilities this position will be responsible for the performance of skilled labor tasks associated with the successful production of events and conversions in assembly facility environment. Skilled Trade Assistant Denver, CO, Colorado Facilities Management & Planning, Maintenance / Grounds / Skilled Trades Skilled individual who installs, maintains and repairs all types of equipment on the DU campus including HVAC, electrical and plumbing systems; in residential houses , dormitory, academic and research facilities. Sign up for job alerts Job Alert 4abc78b5 Finalize your job alert by selecting criteria from the dropdowns below. You can select multiple options from each dropdown by returning to the combobox and re-entering the list of options. Submit at the end to create your job alert. Categories Academic Administration Business Operations / Services Customer Service Academic Administration Business Operations / Services Customer Service Employment Type Staff Full-Time Staff Full-Time Locations Denver, CO, Colorado, United States Denver, CO, Colorado, United States First Name (required) 6b127b57 Last Name (required) c1993ce1 Email (required) f724107a Send Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you Facebook X LinkedIn Instagram Youtube Copyright© 2026 University of Denver. | All rights re